The rule-based phrase matching, exact and loose, both have a hard ceiling. They both have limits because no distance between words or
position of the words can convey the meaning of the phrases. 

The distance threshold approach is unreliable because the phrases will vary for different symptoms; therefore it doesn't always mean that 
two words closer to each other are correct but the words farther away are inccorect.

This limitation can be fixed by integrating Large language model (LLM) as a replacement of the manually written lookup table. 
Since LLMs already know what the sentence means and don't have to teach them what the phrases mean, they would be a smart tool to 
be integrated into the system to take the user's reported symptoms and match it with the 131 dummy symptoms.
